@namespace XLines.Desk.Primitives
@using XLines.Desk.Windowing

@*
    The frame around an inline answer.

    Every snippet gets the same header and the same one way out, so a snippet component only
    ever contains its own content. Without this each of them would re-implement the escape
    hatch, and they would drift — different labels, different placement, some missing it
    entirely, which is how a two-stage pattern quietly becomes a one-stage one.

    The escape sits in the header rather than under the content because a snippet's height
    varies with what it found, and a control that moves down the panel as data arrives is one
    the operator has to look for each time.

    It is on the section-card contract (ADR 0032), in one of two shapes, so a drawer under a row
    and a window's sections read alike:

    - By default the shell IS the card: section.wsection > .wgroup, the name in the card's header
      bar (h3.wgroup__title, the headline voice every card's bar speaks in), the note beside it
      and the escape as the bar's one trailing key, the content as the card's rows. For an answer
      that is rows, a table or a few facts.

    - Cards: the content draws its own cards (several sections, each a FormGroup in a .wflow), so
      the shell must not be a card around them — a card inside a card is the one nesting the
      house rule forbids. The shell is then an unframed pane header over them: the same title in
      the same headline voice and the same trailing key, with no fill and no edge.

    The bar used to be footnote type in secondary ink with a symbol, on a fill with no edge: the
    last frame on the desk that did not read as a card.
*@

@* A region: it appears in response to a press, and announcing it as a named landmark is what
   tells a screen-reader user that something arrived and what it is. It arrives the same way to
   the eye: a short fade down from just above, so the answer reads as coming from the key that
   was pressed rather than having been there all along. *@
<section class="snippet snippet--arrive wsection@(Cards ? " snippet--panes" : null)" id="@Id" role="region" aria-label="@Title">
    @if (Cards)
    {
        @Bar
        <div class="snippet__body">
            @ChildContent
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="wgroup snippet__card">
            @Bar
            <div class="snippet__body">
                @ChildContent
            </div>
        </div>
    }
</section>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>DOM id, so the key that opened this can point at it with aria-controls.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Id { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Kept for callers that name one. A card's bar draws no symbol — a section is named by its
    /// title — so it is not drawn.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string Icon { get; set; } = Icons.Material.Filled.Info;

    /// <summary>
    /// A short qualifier beside the title — coverage, a window, a count. This is where a
    /// snippet states what it is missing rather than letting a row of dashes imply it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Note { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? OpenLabel { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// No delegate hides the escape, for a snippet that is complete on the row. A callback, so
    /// the host that owns it is drawn after it (see <c>RowAction.OpenWindow</c>).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnOpen { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The content draws its own section cards, so the shell is an unframed pane header over them
    /// rather than a card around them (a card in a card is forbidden). Off, the shell is the card.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Cards { get; set; }

    private string OpenText => OpenLabel ?? "Open window";

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    private RenderFragment Bar => __builder =>
    {
        <div class="wgroup__bar snippet__bar">
            <h3 class="wgroup__title snippet__title">@Title</h3>

            @if (Note is not null)
            {
                <span class="snippet__note">@Note</span>
            }

            @if (OnOpen.HasDelegate)
            {
                @* The bar's one trailing key. The word in a span of its own, so a narrow snippet
                   can fold the key to its symbol (desk.css, @container snippet) and keep the word
                   as the key's accessible name; the title says it to the pointer once the word is
                   out of sight. *@
                <div class="wgroup__keys">
                    <button type="button" class="snippet__open" title="@OpenText" @onclick="OnOpen" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
                        <Glyph Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.OpenInNew" /><span class="snippet__open-word">@OpenText</span>
                    </button>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    };
}
